From daeb106b380036bfe5177b3dedb98a9d8a309baa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 08:01:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Raise test_localize_interruptible's child-process start
 timeout

The child process signals `started` as its very first action
(before calling localize()), so this wait is purely process-startup
overhead (fork + reimporting the interpreter/LAL stack), not test
logic. On a riscv64 CI runner -- slower, and possibly contended with
other concurrent jobs -- that startup can take longer than upstream's
5-second budget sized for fast x86 CI, and pytest-rerunfailures'
built-in @pytest.mark.flaky(reruns=5) on this same test already
confirms it: the build failed this exact assertion five times in a
row. Raising the ceiling to 60s costs nothing on fast hardware, since
the wait returns as soon as the event is set.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64 CI is slower than the x86 hardware this timeout was sized for; not a defect on any platform upstream tests]
---
 ligo/skymap/bayestar/tests/test_bayestar.py | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ligo/skymap/bayestar/tests/test_bayestar.py b/ligo/skymap/bayestar/tests/test_bayestar.py
index f11567b..7f6c089 100644
--- a/ligo/skymap/bayestar/tests/test_bayestar.py
+++ b/ligo/skymap/bayestar/tests/test_bayestar.py
@@ -88,7 +88,13 @@ def test_localize_interruptible(mock_event):
         )
         try:
             process.start()
-            assert started.wait(5)
+            # Upstream sizes this for fast x86 CI; process startup (fork +
+            # interpreter/LAL import) can take noticeably longer on a slower,
+            # possibly contended riscv64 CI runner, and the child signals
+            # `started` as its very first action, before doing any real work.
+            # Raising the ceiling costs nothing on fast hardware: the wait
+            # returns as soon as the event is set.
+            assert started.wait(60)
             os.kill(process.pid, signal.SIGINT)
             assert cancelled.wait(10)
         finally:
